{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N6fKF94MRgUyw3Mgb4TE2a","type":"tool_result","content":"330\t        branch's.\n331\t\n332\t    THE HONEST BOUND: this defeats ACCIDENT, not INTENT. Every accidental\n333\t    carrier refuses - a copied message, an amend, a rebase, a cherry-pick, a\n334\t    trailer quoted in an ordinary commit. But forging one deliberately is FOUR\n335\t    git invocations in the lane worktree and no bar at all: `add -A`, `T=$(git\n336\t    write-tree)`, `P=$(git rev-parse <branch>)`, and the `commit` that carries\n337\t    those two values in the trailer. REVIEW-A round 2 drove exactly that and landed an\n338\t    unbarred file on trunk. The format is printed in every refresh commit, so\n339\t    the cost is reading, not reverse-engineering.\n340\t\n341\t    That is accepted, deliberately. The only structural closure is a bar the\n342\t    slot itself runs and cannot skip, and DECISION 3 (owner ruling 2026-07-31)\n343\t    deleted the merge bar outright: a kept-just-in-case bar is exactly the\n344\t    shape §0's governing principle warns against. So the threat model here is\n345\t    the same one the rest of the coordinator holds - bugs, drift and a lane that\n346\t    goes wrong, not a lane that lies on purpose. A lane is trusted code the\n347\t    operator chose to run. If that ever stops being true, the answer is a\n348\t    slot-side bar and a reopened DECISION 3, not a longer trailer.\n349\t    \"\"\"\n350\t    rev = rev or branch\n351\t    message = git_out(root, [\"log\", \"-1\", \"--format=%B\", rev]) or \"\"\n352\t    lines = message.splitlines()\n353\t    if not lines or not lines[0].strip().startswith(refresh_subject(branch)):\n354\t        return None\n355\t    matched = None\n356\t    for line in lines:\n357\t        matched = _ATTEST_RE.match(line.strip())\n358\t        if matched:\n359\t            break\n360\t    if not matched:\n361\t        return None\n362\t    tree, work, summary = matched.group(1), matched.group(2), matched.group(3)\n363\t    if _rev(root, rev + \"^{tree}\") != tree:\n364\t        return None  # the message rode onto a tree it does not describe\n365\t    if _rev(root, rev + \"^1\") != work:\n366\t        return None  # ...or onto a different parent than the one it names\n367\t    return work, summary.strip()\n368\t\n369\t\n370\t# The one non-record tree the MACHINERY itself moves. See\n371\t# `mechanical_close_attestation` for why it is peeled and what bounds that.\n372\t_WORK_PREFIX = b\"docs/work/\"\n373\t_ACTIVE_SPEC = re.compile(rb\"^docs/work/active/([^/]+)/((WI-[0-9]+)-[^/]+[.]md)$\")\n374\t_COMPLETE_SPEC = re.compile(rb\"^docs/work/complete/(WI-[0-9]+-[^/]+[.]md)$\")\n375\t\n376\t\n377\tdef _closed_wi_ids(fields):\n378\t    \"\"\"The canonically ordered ids moved from active/ to complete/, or None.\"\"\"\n379\t    if len(fields) % 2:\n380\t        return None\n381\t    deleted, added, branches = [], [], set()\n382\t    for status, path in zip(fields[::2], fields[1::2]):\n383\t        if not path.startswith(_WORK_PREFIX):\n384\t            return None\n385\t        active = _ACTIVE_SPEC.match(path) if status == b\"D\" else None\n386\t        complete = _COMPLETE_SPEC.match(path) if status == b\"A\" else None\n387\t        if active:\n388\t            branches.add(active.group(1))\n389\t            deleted.append((active.group(2), active.group(3).decode(\"ascii\")))\n390\t        elif complete:\n391\t            added.append(complete.group(1))\n392\t    deleted.sort()\n393\t    if (\n394\t        not deleted\n395\t        or len(branches) != 1\n396\t        or [name for name, _ in deleted] != sorted(added)\n397\t    ):\n398\t        return None\n399\t    return [wi_id for _name, wi_id in deleted]\n400\t\n401\t\n402\tdef mechanical_close_attestation(root, rev):\n403\t    \"\"\"The first parent of `rev` if `rev` is the machinery's OWN adjudication\n404\t    close (`handback.close_adjudication`), else None.\n405\t\n406\t    THE SECOND DISPOSABLE COMMIT, and it is here for the refresh commit's\n407\t    reason. `docs/work/` is deliberately IN the identity — a spec's\n408\t    `safety_class`, `needs` and `Deliverable` are claims a verdict is ABOUT —\n409\t    and the ordering rule that keeps the price low is \"close before the final\n410\t    verdict round\". An adjudication lane CANNOT follow that rule: the round is\n411\t    drawn while the row is still in `active/`, and the close is performed\n412\t    afterwards by the machinery, at the worker's DONE. So the very next commit\n413\t    after an APPROVE moved the spec and staled the round that had just judged\n414\t    it — measured 2026-09-03 on WI-586, whose merge slot then refused \"no\n415\t    logged review round names its current tree\" and needed a hand-compiled\n416\t    legacy rollup (`docs/reviews/WI-586-REVIEW-A.md`).\n417\t\n418\t    WHAT MAKES IT PEELABLE is the same argument the refresh commit makes, not a\n419\t    weaker one. The commit is MACHINE-authored end to end: no agent writes it,\n420\t    its subject is composed by `station.mechanical_close_subject`, and the\n421\t    `## Deliverable` it inserts is a fixed literal\n422\t    (`handback._ADJUDICATION_CLOSE_DELIVERABLE`), so nothing a reviewer could\n423\t    conclude differently about enters the tree. The row's own claims — its\n424\t    `## Dispositions`, which is what the verdict judged — are carried through\n425\t    unchanged.\n426\t\n427\t    VERIFIED AGAINST GIT, not read off the message. The changed-path stream is\n428\t    read with rename detection disabled; it must contain paired deletions from\n429\t    one `docs/work/active/<branch>/` and additions of those same spec names to\n430\t    `docs/work/complete/`. Their canonically ordered filename ids are composed\n431\t    through the writer's `mechanical_close_subject`, and the result must equal\n432\t    the subject exactly. The commit must also have exactly ONE parent (a merge\n433\t    is never this), and every changed path must live under `docs/work/`, which\n434\t    stops a close whose inbound-link relink reached into product or requirement\n435\t    files from being peeled. Each check FAILS TOWARD REVIEW: declining to peel\n436\t    measures at a later rev, so the gate asks for MORE review, never less. The\n437\t    honest bound is `refresh_attestation`'s — this defeats accident and drift,\n438\t    not a lane that lies on purpose, which is the threat model the coordinator\n439\t    holds throughout."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f9fc475e-d3d6-4b26-9e61-40a41045c15b","uuid":"e1d11d5b-f501-492e-abdb-9a9f59bfe391","timestamp":"2026-09-05T02:07:49.770Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/diytechy/Documents/ai-template-drive/wi-595-llr-207-tc-205-return-and-llr/project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/verdict.py","content":"        branch's.\n\n    THE HONEST BOUND: this defeats ACCIDENT, not INTENT. Every accidental\n    carrier refuses - a copied message, an amend, a rebase, a cherry-pick, a\n    trailer quoted in an ordinary commit. But forging one deliberately is FOUR\n    git invocations in the lane worktree and no bar at all: `add -A`, `T=$(git\n    write-tree)`, `P=$(git rev-parse <branch>)`, and the `commit` that carries\n    those two values in the trailer. REVIEW-A round 2 drove exactly that and landed an\n    unbarred file on trunk. The format is printed in every refresh commit, so\n    the cost is reading, not reverse-engineering.\n\n    That is accepted, deliberately. The only structural closure is a bar the\n    slot itself runs and cannot skip, and DECISION 3 (owner ruling 2026-07-31)\n    deleted the merge bar outright: a kept-just-in-case bar is exactly the\n    shape §0's governing principle warns against. So the threat model here is\n    the same one the rest of the coordinator holds - bugs, drift and a lane that\n    goes wrong, not a lane that lies on purpose. A lane is trusted code the\n    operator chose to run. If that ever stops being true, the answer is a\n    slot-side bar and a reopened DECISION 3, not a longer trailer.\n    \"\"\"\n    rev = rev or branch\n    message = git_out(root, [\"log\", \"-1\", \"--format=%B\", rev]) or \"\"\n    lines = message.splitlines()\n    if not lines or not lines[0].strip().startswith(refresh_subject(branch)):\n        return None\n    matched = None\n    for line in lines:\n        matched = _ATTEST_RE.match(line.strip())\n        if matched:\n            break\n    if not matched:\n        return None\n    tree, work, summary = matched.group(1), matched.group(2), matched.group(3)\n    if _rev(root, rev + \"^{tree}\") != tree:\n        return None  # the message rode onto a tree it does not describe\n    if _rev(root, rev + \"^1\") != work:\n        return None  # ...or onto a different parent than the one it names\n    return work, summary.strip()\n\n\n# The one non-record tree the MACHINERY itself moves. See\n# `mechanical_close_attestation` for why it is peeled and what bounds that.\n_WORK_PREFIX = b\"docs/work/\"\n_ACTIVE_SPEC = re.compile(rb\"^docs/work/active/([^/]+)/((WI-[0-9]+)-[^/]+[.]md)$\")\n_COMPLETE_SPEC = re.compile(rb\"^docs/work/complete/(WI-[0-9]+-[^/]+[.]md)$\")\n\n\ndef _closed_wi_ids(fields):\n    \"\"\"The canonically ordered ids moved from active/ to complete/, or None.\"\"\"\n    if len(fields) % 2:\n        return None\n    deleted, added, branches = [], [], set()\n    for status, path in zip(fields[::2], fields[1::2]):\n        if not path.startswith(_WORK_PREFIX):\n            return None\n        active = _ACTIVE_SPEC.match(path) if status == b\"D\" else None\n        complete = _COMPLETE_SPEC.match(path) if status == b\"A\" else None\n        if active:\n            branches.add(active.group(1))\n            deleted.append((active.group(2), active.group(3).decode(\"ascii\")))\n        elif complete:\n            added.append(complete.group(1))\n    deleted.sort()\n    if (\n        not deleted\n        or len(branches) != 1\n        or [name for name, _ in deleted] != sorted(added)\n    ):\n        return None\n    return [wi_id for _name, wi_id in deleted]\n\n\ndef mechanical_close_attestation(root, rev):\n    \"\"\"The first parent of `rev` if `rev` is the machinery's OWN adjudication\n    close (`handback.close_adjudication`), else None.\n\n    THE SECOND DISPOSABLE COMMIT, and it is here for the refresh commit's\n    reason. `docs/work/` is deliberately IN the identity — a spec's\n    `safety_class`, `needs` and `Deliverable` are claims a verdict is ABOUT —\n    and the ordering rule that keeps the price low is \"close before the final\n    verdict round\". An adjudication lane CANNOT follow that rule: the round is\n    drawn while the row is still in `active/`, and the close is performed\n    afterwards by the machinery, at the worker's DONE. So the very next commit\n    after an APPROVE moved the spec and staled the round that had just judged\n    it — measured 2026-09-03 on WI-586, whose merge slot then refused \"no\n    logged review round names its current tree\" and needed a hand-compiled\n    legacy rollup (`docs/reviews/WI-586-REVIEW-A.md`).\n\n    WHAT MAKES IT PEELABLE is the same argument the refresh commit makes, not a\n    weaker one. The commit is MACHINE-authored end to end: no agent writes it,\n    its subject is composed by `station.mechanical_close_subject`, and the\n    `## Deliverable` it inserts is a fixed literal\n    (`handback._ADJUDICATION_CLOSE_DELIVERABLE`), so nothing a reviewer could\n    conclude differently about enters the tree. The row's own claims — its\n    `## Dispositions`, which is what the verdict judged — are carried through\n    unchanged.\n\n    VERIFIED AGAINST GIT, not read off the message. The changed-path stream is\n    read with rename detection disabled; it must contain paired deletions from\n    one `docs/work/active/<branch>/` and additions of those same spec names to\n    `docs/work/complete/`. Their canonically ordered filename ids are composed\n    through the writer's `mechanical_close_subject`, and the result must equal\n    the subject exactly. The commit must also have exactly ONE parent (a merge\n    is never this), and every changed path must live under `docs/work/`, which\n    stops a close whose inbound-link relink reached into product or requirement\n    files from being peeled. Each check FAILS TOWARD REVIEW: declining to peel\n    measures at a later rev, so the gate asks for MORE review, never less. The\n    honest bound is `refresh_attestation`'s — this defeats accident and drift,\n    not a lane that lies on purpose, which is the threat model the coordinator\n    holds throughout.","numLines":110,"startLine":330,"totalLines":913}}}
